WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:11.150
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good morning everybody, and
welcome to this meeting of the Board of NHS

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:12.150
England.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:17.320
We are meeting in London this morning in public,
although of course, it is not in that sense

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.740
a public meeting.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.480
I would like particularly though to welcome
the members of the public who are joining

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.350
us in the meeting room this morning.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:31.980
I have not received any apologies, so I understand
that everybody will be with us this morning.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:37.620
Let me then invite any members of the Board
who have any interests in any of the items

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.710
on the agenda which they might wish to declare.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:44.210
This is over and above, by the way, the general
declarations of interest that we all have.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:49.825
&gt;&gt;John Burn: One thing Malcolm; in addition
to all of my involvements with genomics, have

00:00:49.825 --> 00:00:54.420
accepted a position as Specialist Advisor
to the House of Commons Science and Technology

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:58.690
Committee on Genomics and Gene Editing, but
with an express statement that I will not

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.390
be making any contributions relating to my
NHS role.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.949
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, John, and thank
you for that assurance.

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:09.939
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: In relation to general practice,
I have family members who are working in general

00:01:09.939 --> 00:01:14.780
practice currently at the moment, as well.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.899
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Okay, thanks.

00:01:17.899 --> 00:01:22.990
Just to remind everybody, of course, we run
a rolling list of our interests, which are

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.979
published, and do invite you to update those,
which will be I think tabled in front of you,

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:32.100
if anything has changed since the last time.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.319
I would like particularly to welcome to the
meeting this morning Sue Hill, who will join

00:01:36.319 --> 00:01:41.109
us for our discussion of genomics; and Ros
Roughton, who will join us for our discussion

00:01:41.109 --> 00:02:04.289
of the General Practice Forward View.

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:08.929
Let me then turn, please, to the minutes of
the last meeting.

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:11.560
The Board have received a copy of the minutes.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:17.690
Could I first of all ask you to approve them,
and then secondly, to invite any comments

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.560
from the minutes, of matters arising?

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.560
No, I do not see any, and none have been reported
to me.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.420
May we then move on to the third item on the
agenda, which is the Chairman's Report.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.300
Just a few things which I thought were worthy
of note since our last meeting.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:40.280
First, to report that we have had a formal
accountability meeting with the Department

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.780
of Health, which is, as you know, part of
our constitutional relationship with the Department

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.120
of Health.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:52.380
A part of that meeting was taken up with discussion
around the wording of the mandate.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.750
Negotiations on the mandate continued, and
a formal, final version of that was published

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.120
last week.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.870
That will set the parameters of our work for
the coming year, and the government's expectation

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:09.510
of what we do, and we will of course report
to them both formally, through formal accountability

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:14.830
meetings, but also informally, as the year
proceeds.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:20.590
Secondly, and allied to that, has been the
process for the production of the delivery

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:21.920
plan.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.390
Simon may well say more about that in the
course of his opening comments, and also,

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:31.800
we have an agenda item that relates to it,
but simply to record on behalf of the Board

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:38.730
the very significant amount of work that has
gone on by our team in preparing for a very

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:46.090
significant statement on behalf of the NHS
as to the role of the NHS and its performance

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:47.700
over the coming two years.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:54.551
It has involved quite an amount of negotiation
and discussion with our arm's-length body

00:03:54.551 --> 00:04:01.530
partners, with the Treasury, with Number 10
and with other stakeholders, and we look forward

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:07.010
to publication tomorrow of a statement that
will, again, guide our work over the coming

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.560
two years.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:14.200
On a personal level, simply to report that
I have made visits to some vanguards, including

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.720
with Wendy Becker to the Welwyn Garden City
North East Hertfordshire vanguard for care

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:26.680
homes; to Manchester to look at the work of
'Devo Manc' and the work of the Genomics Service

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:32.689
in Manchester, the Northern Health Sciences
Alliance, the Academic Health Science Network,

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:38.610
and the other organisations in Manchester
that are picking up the baton of devolution

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:45.389
and understanding new ways of working together
across the whole of the conurbation.

00:04:45.389 --> 00:04:52.090
Further meetings with arm's-length bodies,
including NHS Improvement.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:57.930
I have been involved on behalf of this organisation
with what we are currently calling 'Newco',

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:04.080
which is the transition body that picks up
from NHS Property Services and CHP into a

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:11.229
new organisation under the recommendations
of the forthcoming Naylor Report on property

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:18.500
within the NHS, and particularly providing
a new strategic estates capability.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:23.159
Then with Genomics England, and we will come
back to them again this morning, and with

00:05:23.159 --> 00:05:27.159
the Life Sciences Industrial Strategy.

00:05:27.159 --> 00:05:33.520
I would like to pay particular tribute this
morning to Ed Smith, who has announced just

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.630
recently his intention to stand down from
the chairmanship of NHS Improvement.

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:45.539
This I think is a disadvantage to us, because
we know him so well.

00:05:45.539 --> 00:05:50.370
Ed was of course the Vice Chairman here before
he went over to NHS Improvement and, with

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:57.069
the Chief Executive, has built up a wholly
new structure from somewhat unpromising beginnings,

00:05:57.069 --> 00:06:03.300
and to whom we owe a great debt for I think
a strong sense of partnership, which has to

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:08.479
exist across the main national bodies for
the NHS.

00:06:08.479 --> 00:06:13.790
He will continue through to the summer whilst
the search gets underway for his successor,

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:21.550
and we will play, I hope, a helpful role in
identifying the successor and ensuring that

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:25.340
NHSI continues to be well led.

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:31.939
I wish on behalf of the Board to offer our
congratulations to Simon Enright, our Director

00:06:31.939 --> 00:06:37.809
of Comms, who has been named by PR Week as
one of the top ten PR people in the public

00:06:37.809 --> 00:06:39.009
sector.

00:06:39.009 --> 00:06:43.270
He got as high as number two on the list;
I think we all feel that that is probably

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:49.080
not anywhere near high enough, but Simon,
to you go our congratulations, and thanks

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.759
for all that you do for us on what is a critical
part of our business.

00:06:53.759 --> 00:07:00.309
Then finally, simply to remind the Board that
later this morning we are going to be present

00:07:00.309 --> 00:07:06.840
at the awards to our leading staff, the Everyone
Counts awards, in which we recognise and acknowledge

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.479
our staff who have gone the extra mile for
NHS England.

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:15.159
This is an immensely important opportunity
for us to express our thanks for those who

00:07:15.159 --> 00:07:28.339
do so much to make our work successful.

